
====================================================
 PERIPHERAL CONNECTION INTERFACES - DATA TRANSFER
====================================================

1. USB (Universal Serial Bus)
----------------------------------------------------
Speed         : Highest - USB 3.x can reach several Gbps
Convenience   : Plug-and-play, but requires cable + free port
Compatibility : Nearly universal across computers/laptops
Advantage     : Fast, stable, low-latency data transfer
Disadvantage  : Limited by cable length and port availability

2. WI-FI
----------------------------------------------------
Speed         : High but variable - shared with network traffic
Convenience   : No cables, works anywhere on the network
Compatibility : Broad, but depends on matching network protocols
Advantage     : Long range, supports multiple users/devices
Disadvantage  : Interference, congestion, complex setup

3. BLUETOOTH
----------------------------------------------------
Speed         : Lowest - fine for small files, not large scans/video
Convenience   : Easiest quick pairing, no network needed
Compatibility : Good on modern devices, version mismatch issues
Advantage     : Low power use, simple cable-free pairing
Disadvantage  : Short range (under 10m), slow data transfer

====================================================
 QUICK COMPARISON TABLE
====================================================

INTERFACE  | SPEED        | CONVENIENCE          | COMPATIBILITY
-----------+--------------+----------------------+-------------------
USB        | Highest      | Easy setup, tethered | Nearly universal
Wi-Fi      | High/variable| Best for mobility     | Broad, config-needed
Bluetooth  | Lowest       | Best for quick pair   | Good, version-sensitive

